  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/gymnochanda-flamea-2/",
      "html": "<p>Gymnochanda flamea, the red filamentous glassfish, is a dwarf glassfish from Indonesia, up to 4 cm long. All Gymnochanda species (G. filamentosa, G. flamea, G. limi, G. ploegi and G. verae) are pure freshwater fish, and salt should never be added to the water.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/gymnochanda-flamea-2/",
      "html": "<p>G. flamea occurs in South Borneo. Only G. flamea and G. filamentosa (Malaysia, Sumatra (?), West Borneo) have thread-like long dorsal and anal fin rays, red in G. flamea and white-blue in G. filamentosa.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/gymnochanda-flamea-2/",
      "html": "<p>The care of these cute animals, maximum 4 cm long, is not very difficult.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/gymnochanda-flamea-2/",
      "html": "<p>Soft, slightly acidic water (pH 6–7) and a temperature of 24–28°C are recommended.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/gymnochanda-flamea-2/",
      "html": "<p>They only eat live food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/gymnochanda-flamea-2/",
      "html": "<p>Among each other they are very peaceful, even towards other fish, but they should, if at all, only be kept with other tiny and peaceful species.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/gymnochanda-flamea-2/",
      "html": "<p>The animals are egglayers without brood care. The eggs are deposited in fine-grained plants. As far as is known, no successful rearing of the brood under aquarium conditions has been reported so far.</p>"
    }
  ],
